TARGET {
  output: "Entity resolution decision — are two entities the same thing?"
  lang: JSON
  profile: data
}

TOKENS {
  minMatchConfidence: 0.6
  maxMatches: 5
}

RUNTIME_INPUTS {
  | name              | type   | description                                       |
  |-------------------|--------|---------------------------------------------------|
  | new_entity        | object | The newly extracted entity (type, structured, content) |
  | candidates        | array  | Existing entities from the data cube that might match  |
  | entity_type       | string | The entity type being resolved                    |
}

BEHAVIOR ResolveEntity {
  You are determining whether a newly extracted entity matches any existing entity
  in the data cube. This is cross-source entity resolution.

  STEP 1 — Compare key fields.
    For each candidate, compare structured fields that should be unique identifiers:
    - Invoice numbers, order IDs, email addresses, phone numbers → exact match
    - Names → fuzzy match (case-insensitive, handle aliases)
    - Dates → exact match on normalized values
    - Amounts → exact match or within rounding tolerance

  STEP 2 — Score each candidate.
    1.0: Exact identifier match (same invoice number, same email address)
    0.8-0.99: Strong field overlap with no contradictions
    0.6-0.79: Partial match — some fields agree, some missing
    Below 0.6: Not a match — different entities

  STEP 3 — Determine edge type for matches.
    "supersedes" — new entity is a newer version of the existing one
    "references" — new entity mentions or links to the existing one
    "belongs_to" — new entity is a child/component of the existing one
    "related" — entities are connected but neither contains the other

  STEP 4 — Handle contradictions.
    If matching entities have conflicting field values, note the contradiction.
    This does NOT prevent a match — the same invoice from two sources may have different amounts.
    Flag contradictions for the conflict stage.
}

CONSTRAINT {
  MUST compare at least the key identifying fields
  MUST NOT match below minMatchConfidence
  MUST NOT return more than maxMatches matches
  MUST state specific evidence for each match
  MUST flag any field contradictions between matched entities
  MUST NOT assume a match based solely on entity_type
}

CHECKLIST {
  [ ] Each match has a confidence score >= minMatchConfidence
  [ ] Each match has an edge_type
  [ ] Each match has specific evidence (which fields matched)
  [ ] Contradictions between matched entities are flagged
  [ ] No matches based solely on type — specific field evidence required
}

FORMAT {
  OUTPUT: {
    "matches": [
      {
        "candidate_id": "<string>",
        "confidence": <number>,
        "edge_type": "<string>",
        "evidence": "<string>",
        "contradictions": [
          { "field": "<name>", "new_value": "<value>", "existing_value": "<value>" }
        ]
      }
    ]
  }
}
